      "answer": "$20$ and $\\dfrac{20}{3}$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "$\\dfrac{10(1-r^4)}{1-r}\\div\\dfrac{10(1-r^8)}{1-r}=\\dfrac{16}{17}$ — M1* OE; substitute $p,q$ expressions into $\\dfrac{16}{17}$. Statements $16=a(1-r^4)$ and $17=a(1-r^8)$ alone get M0 unless recovered.\nSimplify to $16r^8-17r^4+1=0$, or $\\dfrac{1-r^8}{1-r^4}=1+r^4=\\dfrac{17}{16}$ — DM1.\n$(16r^4-1)(r^4-1)=0\\Rightarrow r=\\pm\\dfrac12$ — A1; equivalently $r^4=\\dfrac1{16}$; condone extra $r=\\pm1$.\n$S_\\infty=\\dfrac{10}{1-(\\pm\\frac12)}$ — DM1; correct sum-to-infinity formula with either their $r$ where $|r|<1$.\n$S_\\infty=20$ and $\\dfrac{20}{3}$ — A1; allow $6.67$ or better; A0 if only one or more than two values.\nTotal: 5",
